# Working principles — {{project_name}}

How code, claims and commits are made in this repository. It applies to every
change, whoever or whatever makes it; editor and assistant configurations
point here rather than restating it.

Each rule says whether it is **enforced** (a gate blocks the commit, the push
or the edit) or **advisory** (expected, reviewed, not blocked).

| Rule | Status | Where it is checked |
|---|---|---|
| No frame reassigned from itself (PIPE002) | enforced | `make gate`: pre-commit hook, Claude edit hook |
| No in-place column mutation (PIPE003) | enforced | same |
| No artifact written without a reported shape (PIPE004) | enforced | same |
| No inline comments (PIPE001) | advisory | reported by `make gate`, blocks only with `--strict` |
| Tests pass, Python and R | enforced | `make test`: pre-commit hook |
| Notes structure and coverage | enforced | `make notes-fast`: pre-commit hook |
| Finding evidence still reproduces its claim | enforced | `make notes`: pre-push hook |
| No AI or co-author attribution in commit messages | enforced | commit-msg hook, Claude Bash hook |
| Commit messages do not refer to a user or agent | enforced (common phrasings) | commit-msg hook |
| Prefer built-in pandas over custom wrappers | advisory | review |
| A test file for every module | advisory | review (a project may add a gate) |
| Config-driven design | advisory | review |
| Empirical falsification of claims | advisory for code, enforced for findings | review; `make notes` |
| One orchestrator: the Makefile, pipeline order in `project.mk` | advisory (enforced for `dvc.yaml`) | review; `make dvc-check` when DVC is used |
| Scripts only orchestrate; functions live in `src/` | advisory | review |
| Library package is data-agnostic; study specifics live in the study package and `config/` | advisory | review |
| Module, function and chain docstrings follow the style guide | advisory | review |

## 1. Pandas as short pipes that report their shape

- A transformation is one method chain: `.pipe()`, `.assign()`, `.query()`,
  `.merge()`, `.rename()`. Never `df = df...`, never `df["col"] = ...`, never
  `inplace=True`.
- Keep chains short. A step that resists piping is doing two things; split it
  into two named functions. One failed attempt is not evidence that it cannot
  be piped.
- Every step reports what it changed: rows in, rows out, delta. Nothing
  writes an artifact (`to_parquet`, `to_csv`, ...) whose shape was not printed.
- When the gate blocks, fix the code. Do not add `# noqa`, do not move the
  file out of the checked directories, do not weaken the checker.

## 2. Prefer built-in pandas

Use what pandas already provides (`value_counts`, `pivot_table`, `merge` with
`indicator=True`, `groupby().agg()` with named aggregations, `pd.cut`,
`Series.str`, `Series.dt`) before writing a helper. A wrapper earns its place
only when it is called from more than one module and says something the
built-in does not. Add a reused helper once, in the package, then call it.

## 3. A test file for every module

Every module `src/<package>/x/y.py` has a test file (`tests/test_x_y.py` or
the project's equivalent), and every R file under the package has a
`test-*.R`. New code ships with its test in the same change. A test counts
only once it has been run and its output seen.

## 4. Notes: decisions, findings, open questions

Notes live in `notes/` as Markdown with front matter. The notes check
validates them and regenerates `notes/INDEX.md`; never edit the index by hand.

```
---
kind: decision | finding | open | reference
title: One line stating the decision, fact or question
covers: path/one.py, path/two.R
claim: exact text the evidence command must print      (finding only)
evidence: shell command run from the repository root   (finding only)
---
Body.
```

What each kind must contain:

- **decision** (`notes/decisions/`): what was decided, why, and the
  alternative that was rejected, written as `Rejected: ...`. One decision per
  file; two decisions may not claim the same covered file.
- **finding** (`notes/findings/`): a fact about the data. `claim` is a string
  the `evidence` command prints; the check re-runs the command and fails when
  the claim no longer appears. A finding without both is rejected.
- **open** (`notes/open/`): an unresolved question and what it blocks.
  Deleted when answered, with the answer moved into a decision or finding.
- **reference**: a pointer to an external source the code depends on.

Every note declares `covers:`. A note that covers nothing cannot be
invalidated and is rejected. Every source file must be covered by some note.
No progress notes: "ran stage 2, it worked" belongs in conversation.

## 5. Empirical falsification

A claim is a hypothesis until a command has tried to break it.

- State what output would prove the claim wrong, run the command, and keep
  the output. "The code looks right" is not evidence.
- Build in small blocks: write, run, see it pass, then move on.
- Data facts become findings whose evidence re-runs; results shown in review
  pages come from executed commands, not transcription.

## 6. Config-driven design

Anything that could reasonably change without the logic changing (paths,
thresholds, label rules, lists of exposures or outcomes, pipeline steps, the
CmdStan release) lives in configuration (`config/*.yaml`, `config/*.csv`,
`project.mk`), not in code. Code reads the config and fails loudly when an
entry is missing. Changing what runs is a config edit and a re-run.

## 7. Commits

- No `Co-Authored-By`, no "Generated with", no AI attribution of any kind.
- Never refer to a person in a commit message: not the user, not an agent,
  not "we" or "I". The message states what the change does.
- Subject as a direct statement: `Drop sub-$2.00 line items at stage 3`, not
  `Per request, I updated the cleaning`.
- Private inputs (`private/`, re-identification maps, credentials) are never
  committed.

## 8. Reproducibility

`make setup` builds everything from pinned files: `environment.yml` (conda),
`renv.lock` (R), `CMDSTAN_VERSION` (CmdStan), `.pre-commit-config.yaml`
(hooks). A new dependency is added by importing it and running `make pin`,
never by installing it ad hoc and leaving the pins stale. See `SETUP.md`.

## 9. Layout: one orchestrator, a library, a study pipeline

```
Makefile, project.mk        the single orchestrator; project.mk holds the stage order
config/                     study configuration: paths, thresholds, rule tables
src/<package>/              library: data-agnostic functions (transforms, models,
                            reporting, tracking); no config, paths or study constants
src/<package>_study/        study pipeline: config loading, source readers, study
                            rules; imports the library, never the reverse
scripts/NN_<stage>.py       orchestration only: load config, call functions in one
                            chain, report shapes, write; no def or class
R/<package>/ (or DESCRIPTION + R/)   R library package; R/run/*.R  R stage runners
correctness/tests/          tests for both packages; library tests use synthetic data
```

- **One orchestrator.** `make all` runs the pipeline; `PIPELINE_PY` and
  `PIPELINE_R` in `project.mk` are the only place the order is written. DVC
  (`dvc.yaml`) or targets may add caching, but each of their stages calls make
  for one entry of that list, so nothing runs that make would not run.
- **Scripts orchestrate.** A stage script reads config, calls library and study
  functions in one chain, reports what changed and writes. A helper needed by
  a script is added to a package and imported, even when only one script uses it.
- **Library and study are separate packages.** The library could be reused by
  another study unchanged: it takes frames and parameters as arguments and has
  tests on synthetic data. What is true only of this study's data (column
  names, labels, exclusions, thresholds) is in `config/` or the study package.
  A function moves from study to library once it no longer names anything
  study-specific.

## 10. Comments and docstrings

The structure is in `legibility/style-guide.md`: a module docstring says what
the module provides (library) or which stage it runs, with a `Chain:` block
listing the steps and a `Reads:`/`Writes:` line (scripts); a function
docstring's first line says what it returns; section labels inside a long
chain are the only comments beyond "why" notes.

